---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import manifest from '../../data/generated/manifest.json';
import { fmtInt } from '../../lib/format.js';
const cityCount = (manifest.places ?? []).length;
---
<Base title="Housing Stress Index — methodology — housinganalytics.org"
      description="How the Housing Stress Index is built: components, weights, national z-scoring, winsorization, and the 0–100 percentile scale. Computed for every U.S. county and city.">
  <section class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/analysis">Analysis</a> · Methodology</p>
    <h1>Housing Stress Index</h1>
    <p>
      The Housing Stress Index (HSI) condenses five housing-affordability measures into a single
      <strong>0–100 score</strong>, where higher means more stressed. It is computed independently for all
      {fmtInt(manifest.county_count)} U.S. counties and all {fmtInt(cityCount)} cities and towns over
      5,000 people, and it appears on the rankings tables, the maps, and every county and city profile.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read it</h2>
    <p>
      The score is a <strong>national percentile within its own universe</strong>. A county scoring 82 is
      more housing-stressed than 82% of U.S. counties; a city scoring 82 is more stressed than 82% of
      cities. Because counties and cities are ranked against different sets, a county’s 82 and a city’s 82
      are <em>not</em> directly comparable. Each place also carries a <strong>rank</strong> — rank 1 is the
      single most-stressed county (or city).
    </p>

    <h2>Components</h2>
    <p>
      Five measures, each already published on the profiles, each oriented so that a higher value means
      more stress. All are from the ACS 5-year estimates.
    </p>
    <table class="method-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Weight</th><th>What it captures</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Renter cost burden (30%+ of income)</td><td>0.28</td><td>Core affordability pressure on renters</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Renter severe cost burden (50%+)</td><td>0.14</td><td>Depth of acute renter stress</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Owner cost burden (30%+ of income)</td><td>0.14</td><td>Affordability pressure on owners</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rent-to-income ratio</td><td>0.22</td><td>Market rent relative to local incomes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price-to-income ratio</td><td>0.22</td><td>Home prices relative to local incomes</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Method</h2>
    <ol class="method-steps">
      <li><strong>Standardize.</strong> Each component is converted to a national <em>z-score</em> across
        all places in its universe (counties, or cities) — its distance from the national mean in standard
        deviations. This puts percentages and ratios on one comparable scale.</li>
      <li><strong>Winsorize.</strong> Each z-score is clipped to ±3, so a single small-sample extreme
        cannot dominate the result. This matters most for the smallest counties, whose ACS estimates carry
        large sampling error.</li>
      <li><strong>Weighted sum.</strong> The clipped z-scores are combined using the weights above into a
        single composite value. A place missing a component contributes a neutral zero for it.</li>
      <li><strong>Percentile &amp; rank.</strong> The composite is converted to a 0–100 national
        percentile (100 = most stressed) and a dense rank (1 = most stressed). Percentile — not a raw
        min–max rescale — so a handful of extreme places cannot compress everyone else into a narrow band.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What it deliberately leaves out</h2>
    <p>
      Two measures that an affordability index might plausibly include are excluded on purpose, and the
      exclusions are recorded in the published data:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Vacancy rate</strong> — directionally ambiguous. High vacancy can signal distress and
        abandonment <em>or</em> slack, cheaper supply; low vacancy signals a tight, stressed market.
        Including it would muddy the interpretation of the score.</li>
      <li><strong>Owner-occupancy trend</strong> — requires multiple ACS vintages, which this
        cross-sectional index does not yet use. It is a candidate for a future trend-aware revision.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      The original v4 concept for this index also listed vacancy and an owner-occupancy change term. Both
      were dropped during the build for the reasons above; this page is the record of that decision.
    </p>

    <h2>Caveats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It is a relative measure.</strong> A score is a percentile within counties or within
        cities — it says where a place sits <em>among its peers</em>, not against a fixed threshold. If
        national housing stress rose everywhere, the ranking could barely move.</li>
      <li><strong>Small samples.</strong> The smallest counties carry larger ACS sampling error; the index
        flags a place <code>low_confidence</code> when it has fewer than three of the five components, or
        (for counties) a population under 1,000. The cities universe has no places under 5,000 people, so
        this rarely applies there.</li>
      <li><strong>Component overlap.</strong> Renter cost burden (30%+) and severe renter cost burden
        (50%+) overlap by construction; severe burden is weighted separately to emphasize acute stress.</li>
      <li><strong>The weights are a judgment.</strong> They reflect a defensible default, not a law of
        nature. They are documented here precisely so the choice is open to scrutiny.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
    <p>
      The index is computed at build time — <code>scripts/build-index.mjs</code> for counties and
      <code>scripts/build-index-places.mjs</code> for cities — with every weight in a single block at the
      top of each script. The full per-place breakdown, including each component’s z-score and its
      contribution to the composite, is emitted alongside the site data, so any score can be traced back
      to its inputs.
    </p>

    <aside class="disclaimer" role="note" aria-label="Disclaimer">
      <p class="disclaimer__label">Disclaimer</p>
      <p class="disclaimer__body">
        The Housing Stress Index is a composite measure provided for informational purposes only. It is
        derived from ACS 5-year estimates but is not independently validated by Economic Impact Group, LLC,
        and reflects deliberate methodological choices described above. Consult the original ACS sources for
        decisions that depend on precise figures.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <style>
    .method-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-6); font-size: var(--fs-sm); }
    .method-table th, .method-table td { text-align: left; padding: .5rem .6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-sage, #d6dbd4); vertical-align: top; }
    .method-table th { font-size: var(--fs-xs); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; }
    .method-table td:nth-child(2), .method-table th:nth-child(2) { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
    .method-steps { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-6); padding-left: 1.2rem; }
    .method-steps li { padding-left: .3rem; }
    .note { font-size: var(--fs-sm); opacity: .8; border-left: 3px solid var(--color-gold, #f19623); padding-left: var(--space-3); }
    code { background: rgba(0,0,0,.05); padding: .05em .35em; border-radius: 4px; font-size: .9em; }
  </style>
</Base>
